EXT. DEAD MULE CREEK - HOMESTEAD PORCH - DAWN

A sagging cabin of sun-bleached cedar. A rusted washtub. A windmill ticks in the thin wind. The prairie runs flat to the horizon, gold and empty.

SHERIFF AMOS TULL, 60s, gray mustache, dusty duster, sits on the top step with his hat in his hands. JOSEPHINE CRANE, 40s, sunburned, in a man's shirt and canvas trousers, stands at the screen door with a shotgun cradled like a baby.

AMOS
Morning, Josephine.

JOSEPHINE
Sheriff.

AMOS
Coffee on?

JOSEPHINE
Not for you.

AMOS
Fair enough.

JOSEPHINE
You ride all night?

AMOS
Most of it.

JOSEPHINE
For what?

AMOS
For your husband.

JOSEPHINE
He's not here.

AMOS
I know. I found his horse at the creek.

JOSEPHINE
That mare throws riders.

AMOS
She does. Wasn't thrown, though. Wasn't anything. Just standing there, saddle empty.

JOSEPHINE
Then he walked off.

AMOS
Without his boots?

JOSEPHINE
He doesn't like boots.

AMOS
Josephine.

JOSEPHINE
I said he doesn't like boots.

AMOS
I'm too old and too tired to argue. Is he in the cellar?

JOSEPHINE
(after a beat)
No.

AMOS
The well, then.

She doesn't answer. The windmill ticks. He rises, puts his hat on, and she lowers the shotgun until the barrels touch the porch boards.
